<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abraham-lincoln.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abraham Lincoln</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abraham Lincoln was the 16th president of the United States, from 1861 until his assassination in 1865.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abraham-roentgen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abraham-roentgen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abraham Roentgen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abraham Roentgen was a German cabinetmaker who set up a furniture workshop at Neuwied on the Rhine in 1750.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abraham-van-peere"><span class="enc-name">Abraham van Peere</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abraham van Peere was a merchant of Vlissingen in Zeeland who founded the Dutch colony of Berbice in present-day Guyana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abruzzo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abruzzo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Abruzzo is a region of central Italy between the Apennines and the Adriatic Sea, with its capital at L'Aquila.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/absalom"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/absalom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Absalom</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Absalom is the third son of King David in the Hebrew Bible, born to Maacah, daughter of Talmai, king of Geshur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/absalon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/absalon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Absalon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Absalon was a Danish churchman and statesman, Bishop of Roskilde and later Archbishop of Lund, and the chief adviser of King Valdemar I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/absheron-peninsula"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/absheron-peninsula.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Absheron Peninsula</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Absheron Peninsula is a peninsula in eastern Azerbaijan that juts some 60 kilometres into the Caspian Sea, with the capital Baku on its southern coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abtai-sain-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abtai-sain-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abtai Sain Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abtai Sain Khan was a Khalkha Mongol prince, a great-grandson of Dayan Khan, who became the leading ruler of central Khalkha in the late 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-al-shii"><span class="enc-name">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i was an Ismaili missionary who converted and organised the Kutama Berbers of what is now Algeria in the late ninth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-muhammad-afrighid"><span class="enc-name">Abu Abdallah Muhammad (Afrighid)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Abdallah Muhammad was the last Afrighid shah of Khwarazm, ruling from Kath on the lower Amu Darya in the late 10th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-abbas-ahmad-ii"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Abbas Ahmad II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Abbas Ahmad II was a Hafsid sultan who ruled from Tunis from 1370 to 1394.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-abbas-al-saffah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-al-abbas-al-saffah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah was the first caliph of the Abbasid Caliphate, reigning from 750 to 754.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-baqa-khalid"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Baqa Khalid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Baqa Khalid was a Hafsid prince who ruled the Hafsid emirate of Bejaia from 1301, succeeding his father Abu Zakariya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fath-dawud"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Fath Dawud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Fath Dawud was the Ismaili ruler of Multan in the early 11th century, of the dynasty that had held the city since the late 10th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fath-khan-zand"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Fath Khan Zand</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Fath Khan Zand was a son of Karim Khan Zand, the founder of the Zand Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Fida</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Fida was an Ayyubid prince, historian and geographer who ruled Hama under Mamluk overlordship from 1310 until his death in 1331.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-hakam-ibn-hassun"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Hakam ibn Hassun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Hakam ibn Hassun was the qadi of Málaga who ruled the city as an independent taifa from 1145, when Andalusi cities expelled their Almoravid garrisons.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-hasan-ali-ibn-othman"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-al-hasan-ali-ibn-othman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Othman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Othman was the Marinid sultan of Morocco from 1331 to 1348 and the dynasty's most ambitious ruler.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-hasan-ali-of-granada"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Hasan Ali of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Hasan Ali, known to the Castilians as Muley Hacén, was Nasrid emir of Granada from 1464 to 1482 and again in 1483–1485.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-hazm-ibn-jahwar"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Hazm ibn Jahwar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Hazm ibn Jahwar was a Córdoban vizier who became head of the Taifa of Córdoba in 1031, when the city's notables deposed Hisham III and abolished the Caliphate of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-khattab-al-maafiri"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Khattab al-Ma'afiri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Khattab al-Ma'afiri was an Arab Ibadi leader elected imam in 757 by the Ibadi Berbers of Tripolitania and the Jebel Nafusa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-makarim-hibat-allah"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Makarim Hibat Allah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Makarim Hibat Allah was the chief of an Arab tribal group of the Rabi'a settled on Egypt's southern frontier around Aswan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-misk-kafur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-al-misk-kafur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Misk Kafur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Misk Kafur was a eunuch of African origin, once a slave, who became ruler of Ikhshidid Egypt.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-muhajir-dinar"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Muhajir Dinar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Muhajir Dinar was an Umayyad commander and governor of Ifriqiya from about 674 to 681.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-qasim-muhammad-ibn-abbad"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Qasim Muhammad ibn Abbad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Qasim Muhammad ibn Abbad was the qadi of Seville who founded the Abbadid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-qasim-samgu"><span class="enc-name">Abu al-Qasim Samgu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu al-Qasim Samgu was a leader of the Miknasa Berbers and a Sufri Kharijite who became ruler of Sijilmasa after the deposition of its first leader, Isa ibn Yazid, in…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-ali-al-hasan-ibn-marwan"><span class="enc-name">Abu Ali al-Hasan ibn Marwan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Ali al-Hasan ibn Marwan was the founder of Marwanid rule in Diyar Bakr, in what is now south-eastern Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-amr-al-dani"><span class="enc-name">Abu Amr al-Dani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Amr al-Dani (981–1053) was an Andalusi scholar of the Quran, born in Córdoba, who settled in Dénia under its ruler Mujahid al-Amiri.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-amr-uthman"><span class="enc-name">Abu Amr Uthman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Amr Uthman was the Hafsid sultan of Ifriqiya from 1435 to 1488, one of the longest reigns of the dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakar-of-johor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-bakar-of-johor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Bakar of Johor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Bakar of Johor was temenggung of Johor from 1862, maharaja from 1868 and sultan from 1886.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ahmad-ibn-tahir"><span class="enc-name">Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Tahir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Tahir was the head of the Banu Tahir, a local family who took effective control of the Taifa of Murcia in the 11th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-al-siddiq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-bakr-al-siddiq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Bakr al-Siddiq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Bakr al-Siddiq was a close companion of the Prophet Muhammad and the first caliph of the Rashidun Caliphate, ruling from 632 to 634.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-atiku"><span class="enc-name">Abu Bakr Atiku</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Bakr Atiku was the second caliph of Sokoto, ruling from 1837 to 1842.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-muhammad"><span class="enc-name">Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad was a sultan of the Walashma dynasty of the Adal Sultanate in the early sixteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-sad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-bakr-ibn-sad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd was atabeg of Fars of the Salghurid dynasty from 1226 to 1260.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-shaykh-umar"><span class="enc-name">Abu Bakr ibn Shaykh Umar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Bakr ibn Shaykh Umar was the sultan of Mogadishu when the Moroccan traveller Ibn Battuta visited the city in 1331.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-umar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-bakr-ibn-umar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Bakr ibn Umar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Bakr ibn Umar was an Almoravid leader of the Lamtuna Berbers in the eleventh century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-tihrani"><span class="enc-name">Abu Bakr Tihrani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Bakr Tihrani was a fifteenth-century Persian historian at the court of the Aq Qoyunlu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-dhabi-investment-authority"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-dhabi-investment-authority.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Dhabi Investment Authority</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Abu Dhabi Investment Authority is the sovereign wealth fund of the emirate of Abu Dhabi, founded in 1976 to invest the surplus revenues of its oil exports abroad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-dulaf"><span class="enc-name">Abu Dulaf</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Dulaf al-Qasim ibn Isa was an Arab general and poet of the Banu Ijl who served the Abbasid caliphs Harun al-Rashid and al-Ma'mun.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-faris-abd-al-aziz-ii"><span class="enc-name">Abu Faris Abd al-Aziz II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Faris Abd al-Aziz II was a Hafsid sultan who ruled from Tunis from 1394 to 1434, one of the longest and most successful reigns of the dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-firas-al-hamdani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-firas-al-hamdani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Firas al-Hamdani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Firas al-Hamdani was an Arab poet and warrior, a cousin of Sayf al-Dawla, the Hamdanid emir of Aleppo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-fudayk"><span class="enc-name">Abu Fudayk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Fudayk, Abd Allah ibn Thawr, was a Kharijite leader of the Najdiyya movement in eastern Arabia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-hafs-umar-al-hintati"><span class="enc-name">Abu Hafs Umar al-Hintati</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Hafs Umar al-Hintati was a Masmuda Berber chief and one of the first companions of Ibn Tumart, founder of the Almohad movement.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-hafs-umar-al-iqritishi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-hafs-umar-al-iqritishi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Hafs Umar al-Iqritishi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Hafs Umar al-Iqritishi led the Andalusi exiles who had rebelled against the Umayyad emir of Córdoba and later held Alexandria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-hafs-umar-i"><span class="enc-name">Abu Hafs Umar I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Hafs Umar I was the Hafsid sultan of Tunis from 1284 to 1295, a son of Abu Zakariya Yahya I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-hamid-al-gharnati"><span class="enc-name">Abu Hamid al-Gharnati</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Hamid al-Gharnati was an Andalusian Muslim traveller born in Granada who journeyed through North Africa, the Middle East and eastern Europe in the twelfth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-hammu-musa-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-hammu-musa-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Hammu Musa II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Hammu Musa II was sultan of the Zayyanid Kingdom of Tlemcen from 1359 to 1389.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-hamza-al-mukhtar-ibn-awf"><span class="enc-name">Abu Hamza al-Mukhtar ibn Awf</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Hamza al-Mukhtar ibn Awf was an Ibadi Kharijite leader and the general of Abd Allah ibn Yahya, the imam proclaimed in Hadramawt in 746.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-hassun-al-semlali"><span class="enc-name">Abu Hassun al-Semlali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Hassun al-Semlali was a sharif and descendant of the saint Sidi Ahmed ou Moussa who built a principality in the Tazeroualt region of the Anti-Atlas in southern…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-imran-al-fasi"><span class="enc-name">Abu Imran al-Fasi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Imran al-Fasi was a Moroccan Maliki jurist who taught in Kairouan in the early eleventh century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-inan-faris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Inan Faris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Inan Faris was sultan of the Marinid dynasty of Morocco from 1348 to 1358.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-isa-ibn-labbun"><span class="enc-name">Abu Isa ibn Labbun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Isa ibn Labbun was a Valencian notable who made himself master of Murviedro, the ancient Saguntum, after al-Qadir was installed in Valencia as a Castilian client in…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-ishaq-al-sahili"><span class="enc-name">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili was an Andalusi poet from Granada who accompanied Mansa Musa back from Mecca to the Mali Empire in the 1320s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-ishaq-ibrahim-i"><span class="enc-name">Abu Ishaq Ibrahim I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Ishaq Ibrahim I was Hafsid sultan of Ifriqiya from 1279 to 1283, a son of Abu Zakariya Yahya I and brother of al-Mustansir.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-ishaq-inju"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-ishaq-inju.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Ishaq Inju</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Ishaq Inju was the last Injuid ruler of Fars, governing from Shiraz from about 1343.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-ja"><span class="enc-name">Abu Ja</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Ja, also called Jatau, was a ruler of the Hausa dynasty of Zazzau driven into exile by the Fulani jihad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-kalijar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-kalijar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Kalijar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Kalijar was a Buyid ruler who governed Fars and Khuzistan from 1024 and also Iraq from 1044 until his death in 1048.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-kamal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-kamal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Kamal</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Kamal (al-Bukamal) is a town in eastern Syria, in Deir ez-Zor Governorate, on the Euphrates at the border with Iraq, opposite al-Qa'im.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-mansur-fulad-sutun"><span class="enc-name">Abu Mansur Fulad Sutun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Mansur Fulad Sutun was the last Buyid ruler of Fars, a son of Abu Kalijar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-muhammad-abd-al-wahid-ibn-abi-hafs"><span class="enc-name">Abu Muhammad Abd al-Wahid ibn Abi Hafs</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Muhammad Abd al-Wahid ibn Abi Hafs was an Almohad governor of Ifriqiya and a son of Abu Hafs Umar, a companion of the Almohad founder Ibn Tumart.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-musa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-musa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Musa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Musa is an island in the Persian Gulf near the Strait of Hormuz, long administered by Sharjah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-muslim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Muslim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Muslim was the Persian revolutionary leader who organised the Abbasid movement in Khurasan and led the uprising against the Umayyad Caliphate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-nasr-mansur"><span class="enc-name">Abu Nasr Mansur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Nasr Mansur ibn Iraq was a Persian mathematician and astronomer, a prince of the Afrighid dynasty of Khwarazm.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-numayy-ii"><span class="enc-name">Abu Numayy II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Numayy II was Sharif of Mecca, sharing rule with his father from 1512 and governing alone from 1525 until his death in 1584.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-nur-hilal-ibn-abi-qurra"><span class="enc-name">Abu Nur Hilal ibn Abi Qurra</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Nur Hilal ibn Abi Qurra was a chief of the Banu Ifran, a Zanata Berber clan, who made himself independent at Ronda around 1015, at first as a governor for the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-qurra"><span class="enc-name">Abu Qurra</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Qurra was the leader of the Banu Ifran, a Zenata Berber tribe of the central Maghreb, during the Berber revolts against Arab rule.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-rakwa"><span class="enc-name">Abu Rakwa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Rakwa was an Umayyad pretender who led a major revolt against the Fatimid Caliphate from 1004 to 1006.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-al-jannabi"><span class="enc-name">Abu Sa'id al-Jannabi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Sa'id al-Jannabi was an Ismaili missionary who founded the Qarmatian state of Bahrain in eastern Arabia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-bahadur-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-said-bahadur-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Sa'id Bahadur Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Sa'id Bahadur Khan was the last effective ruler of the Ilkhanate, the Mongol state centred on Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-mirza"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-said-mirza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Sa'id Mirza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Sa'id Mirza (1424–1469) was a Timurid ruler, a great-grandson of Timur, who seized Samarkand in 1451 with the help of the Uzbek khan Abu'l-Khayr and later took…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-salabikh"><span class="enc-name">Abu Salabikh</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Salabikh is an archaeological site in southern Iraq, about 20 kilometres north-west of Nippur, whose ancient name is uncertain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-simbel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Simbel</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Simbel is a site on the Nile in Lower Nubia, southern Egypt, with two rock-cut temples built by Ramesses II in the 13th century BCE; the larger is fronted by four…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-taghlib"><span class="enc-name">Abu Taghlib</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Taghlib was the Hamdanid emir of Mosul who deposed his father Nasir al-Dawla in 967.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-tahir-al-jannabi"><span class="enc-name">Abu Tahir al-Jannabi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Tahir al-Jannabi was ruler of the Qarmatian state of Bahrain in eastern Arabia from 923 until his death in 944.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-tahir-ibn-muhammad"><span class="enc-name">Abu Tahir ibn Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Tahir ibn Muhammad was the founder of the Hazaraspid dynasty of Luristan in south-western Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-ubayda-ibn-al-jarrah"><span class="enc-name">Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah was an early companion of Muhammad and one of the leading commanders of the Rashidun conquest of Syria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-uthman-said-ibn-hakam"><span class="enc-name">Abu Uthman Said ibn Hakam</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Uthman Said ibn Hakam was ra'is of Muslim Menorca from 1234 until his death in 1282, ruling from Madina Manurqa, the later Ciutadella, as a tributary of the Crown…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I was the second caliph of the Almohad Caliphate, son and successor of Abd al-Mu'min.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-yusuf-yaqub-ibn-abd-al-haqq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-yusuf-yaqub-ibn-abd-al-haqq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu Yusuf Yaqub ibn Abd al-Haqq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Yusuf Yaqub ibn Abd al-Haqq was sultan of the Marinid dynasty of Morocco from 1258 to 1286.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-zakariya-yahya-i"><span class="enc-name">Abu Zakariya Yahya I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Zakariya Yahya I was the founder of the Hafsid Sultanate of Tunis.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-zayd-almohad-governor-of-valencia"><span class="enc-name">Abu Zayd (Almohad governor of Valencia)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu Zayd was an Almohad prince, a descendant of the caliph Abd al-Mu'min, and the last Almohad governor of Valencia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-abbas-mamun"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Abbas Ma'mun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Abbas Ma'mun, Ma'mun II, was Khwarazmshah of the Ma'munid dynasty from 1009 to 1017, ruling from Gurganj.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-aswar-shavur-i"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Aswar Shavur I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Aswar Shavur I was the Shaddadid emir of Ganja in the south Caucasus from 1049 to 1067, having earlier ruled Dvin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-fadl-nasr-ibn-ahmad"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Fadl Nasr ibn Ahmad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Fadl Nasr ibn Ahmad was the founder of the Nasrid line of maliks of Sistan, a branch of the Saffarid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-fath-muhammad-ibn-annaz"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Fath Muhammad ibn Annaz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Fath Muhammad ibn Annaz was a Kurdish chief of the Shadhanjan tribe who founded the Annazid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-bayhaqi"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Fazl Bayhaqi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Fazl Bayhaqi was a Persian secretary in the chancery of the Ghaznavid sultans and a historian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak was the vizier, historian and close adviser of the Mughal emperor Akbar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-futuh-al-hasan-ibn-jafar"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Futuh al-Hasan ibn Ja'far</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Futuh al-Hasan ibn Ja'far was Sharif of Mecca from about 994 to 1039.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-ghazi-bahadur"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Ghazi Bahadur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Ghazi Bahadur was khan of Khiva from about 1643 to 1663 and a noted historian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-harith-muhammad"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Harith Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Harith Muhammad was the Farighunid amir of Guzgan, in present-day northern Afghanistan, in the late 10th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-hayja-abdallah-ibn-hamdan"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Hayja Abdallah ibn Hamdan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Hayja Abdallah ibn Hamdan was an Arab commander of the Banu Taghlib who laid the foundations of the Hamdanid dynasty's power.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-hayja-wahsudan"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Hayja Wahsudan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Hayja Wahsudan was the Rawwadid emir of Azerbaijan, ruling north-western Iran from Tabriz from about 1019 to 1059.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abul-khayr-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Khayr Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Khayr Khan was a Shibanid descendant of Genghis Khan who ruled the nomadic Uzbeks of the steppe east of the Ural River from 1428 until his death.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-shawk-faris"><span class="enc-name">Abu'l-Shawk Faris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abu'l-Shawk Faris was the leading ruler of the Annazid dynasty, Kurdish lords of the Zagros passes between Iraq and Iran, from 1011 to 1046.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-garbai"><span class="enc-name">Abubakar Garbai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abubakar Garbai was Shehu of Borno from 1902 to 1922.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-tafawa-balewa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abubakar-tafawa-balewa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abubakar Tafawa Balewa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abubakar Tafawa Balewa was a Nigerian politician of the Northern People's Congress who became federal prime minister in 1957 and led Nigeria to independence on 1…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-hasan-qutb-shah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abul-hasan-qutb-shah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abul Hasan Qutb Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abul Hasan Qutb Shah, known as Tana Shah, was the last sultan of Golconda in the Deccan, reigning from 1672 to 1687.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-abbas"><span class="enc-name">Abul-Abbas</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Abul-Abbas was an Asian elephant sent by the Abbasid caliph Harun al-Rashid to Charlemagne.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abulkhair-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abulkhair-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abulkhair Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abulkhair Khan was khan of the Junior Juz, the western Kazakh horde, from about 1718.</span></a></li></ul>
